arXiv:2509.07484v1 [cs.CV] 09 Sep 2025</div>
</div><div class="ltx_page_content">
<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">LINR Bridge: Vector Graphic Animation via Neural Implicits and Video Diffusion Priors<span class="ltx_pubnotes"><span class="ltx_pubnotes_content"><span class="ltx_pubnote ltx_role_thanks"><span class="ltx_note_name">Thanks: </span><math id="m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ast" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo mathsize="0.800em">∗</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ast</annotation></semantics></math><span id="id1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;"> Corresponding author. This work was supported in part by the National Natural Science Foundation of China under Grant 62471009, in part by CCF-Tencent Rhino-Bird Open Research Fund, in part by the Key Laboratory of Science, Technology and Standard in Press Industry (Key Laboratory of Intelligent Press Media Technology), and in part by The Fundamental Research Funds for the Central Universities, Peking University.</span></span></span></span></h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Wenshuo Gao, Xicheng Lan, Luyao Zhang, Shuai Yang*
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Wangxuan Institute of Computer Technology, State Key Laboratory of Multimedia Information Processing, 
<br class="ltx_break">Peking University, Beijing, China
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Vector graphics, known for their scalability and user-friendliness, provide a unique approach to visual content compared to traditional pixel-based images. Animation of these graphics, driven by the motion of their elements, offers enhanced comprehensibility and controllability but often requires substantial manual effort. To automate this process, we propose a novel method that integrates implicit neural representations with text-to-video diffusion models for vector graphic animation. Our approach employs layered implicit neural representations to reconstruct vector graphics, preserving their inherent properties such as infinite resolution and precise color and shape constraints, which effectively bridges the large domain gap between vector graphics and diffusion models. The neural representations are then optimized using video score distillation sampling, which leverages motion priors from pretrained text-to-video diffusion models. Finally, the vector graphics are warped to match the representations resulting in smooth animation. Experimental results validate the effectiveness of our method in generating vivid and natural vector graphic animations, demonstrating significant improvement over existing techniques that suffer from limitations in flexibility and animation quality.</p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_keywords">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_keywords">Index Terms: </h6>Vector Graphics, Implicit Neural Representation, Diffusion Model, Score Distillation Sampling

</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">I </span><span id="S1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Introduction</span></h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Vector graphics are a widely used form of flat visual content, distinct from traditional pixel-based raster images. They consist of defined elements such as circles, squares, lines, and Bézier curves, and are known for their simplicity, flatness, resolution-independent scalability, and user-friendliness. Consequently, vector graphics are extensively applied in icon design, animation, posters, and web design. Scalable Vector Graphics (SVG) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite> is a popular XML-based format for representing them.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Unlike raster images, whose animations rely on pixel color changes, vector graphic animations are achieved by transforming graphic elements, offering greater clarity and control. However, producing smooth and coordinated motion often demands considerable manual effort. Typically, the process involves constructing a skeleton and applying transformations accordingly.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">This highlights the need for automated vector graphic animation systems. Recently, diffusion-based video generation models such as ModelScope <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>, VideoCrafter2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>, DynamiCrafter <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>, I2VGen-XL <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>, SVD <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>, and CogVideoX <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite> have gained rapid traction. One approach is to render vector graphics into raster images and animate them using these pretrained models with text prompts. However, due to their training on natural imagery and lack of shape/color constraints, the results are often unstable and inconsistent with expectations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>, <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>. Therefore, a dedicated system for vector graphic animation is essential.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Although some methods for vector graphic animation have been proposed <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>, <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>, they face notable limitations. LiveSketch <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite> directly optimizes SVG point parameters to animate sketches, but it is limited to global transformations and point-level local shifts, offering little control over shapes and often causing structural distortion and unpredictable motion. AniClipart <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite> extracts SVG skeletons and applies As-Rigid-As-Possible (ARAP) deformation for cartoon animation. However, it depends heavily on instance-specific keypoints and skeletal movement, enforcing overly rigid constraints that result in stiff animations.
Furthermore, both methods render parameterized SVGs into raster images before applying diffusion model optimization via video score distillation sampling (VSDS) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>. This rasterization introduces a significant domain gap, complicating optimization and limiting the effectiveness of diffusion priors—ultimately preventing natural SVG animation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">This domain gap motivated our research. We observed that the implicit neural representation (INR) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>, <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>, which is introduced and widely adopted in image and 3D modeling, is highly effective in reconstructing and animating vector graphics. INR models an image using a neural network, typically taking coordinate position information as input and producing the corresponding color as output. INR has several advantageous characteristics. By using coordinates as input, INR constructs images with infinite resolution, which aligns well with the nature of SVG. Moreover, INR offers shape manipulability, significantly bridging the domain gap between parameterized SVG and rasterized images.
Our findings suggest that our color-wise layered implicit neural representation (LINR) can effectively reconstruct vector graphics, as they consist of various shapes, each assigned a specific color, enabling easier and more comprehensible animation.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.07484v1/pipeline.png" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/183;" width="476" height="183" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 1: </span>The pipeline of <span id="S1.F1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">LINR Bridge</span> takes an SVG <math id="S1.F1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> and a text prompt <math id="S1.F1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> as inputs, and produces an SVG animation as output. The pipeline consists of three steps: (1) <span id="S1.F1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Vector Graphics Reconstruction</span>: Optimize a LINR network <math id="S1.F1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> to reconstruct the input SVG <math id="S1.F1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>. (2) <span id="S1.F1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Coarse Animation Generation</span>: Replicate the network <math id="S1.F1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="S1.F1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> times to construct a <math id="S1.F1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>-frames static initial video <math id="S1.F1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{f}</annotation></semantics></math>. Input the frames rendered from <math id="S1.F1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{f}</annotation></semantics></math> along with the text prompt <math id="S1.F1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> into a text-to-video (T2V) diffusion model and optimize using VSDS to obtain a coarse LINR animation. (3) <span id="S1.F1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Animation Refinement</span>: Warp <math id="S1.F1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> based on optical flow or directly optimize <math id="S1.F1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> to match the LINR animation, resulting in the final animated SVG <math id="S1.F1.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐬</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{s}</annotation></semantics></math>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we propose <span id="S1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">LINR Bridge</span>, a novel coarse-to-fine framework for vector graphic animation that utilizes LINR to bridge the domain gap between vector graphic representations and existing video diffusion models. Specifically, we first reconstruct the input vector graphics using LINR. This LINR is then replicated across the video frame dimension and optimized using VSDS to generate a coarse LINR animation. The input vector graphics are warped based on optical flow or directly optimized to match the LINR animation, resulting in the final vector graphic animation. LINR effectively constrains the color and shape of the images, while the VSDS loss introduces motion priors from the pretrained video diffusion models.
Experiments show that our method generates vivid and natural vector graphic animations.
Our contributions are summarized as follows:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We propose an effective method using LINR to reconstruct vector graphics, resulting in high reconstruction quality, well-preserved shapes, and ease of optimization.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We combine LINR with VSDS for animating vector graphics. LINR serves as an intermediate representation to bridge the domain gap between parameterized SVG and raster images, empowering effective motion prior distillation from text-to-video (T2V) diffusion models.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">By warping or optimizing SVG to match the intermediate LINR animation, we achieve complete and smooth SVG animation with appropriate constraints and flexibility.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S1.p6.2" class="ltx_p">The rest of the paper is organized as follows. Sec. <a href="#S2" title="II Proposed Method ‣ LINR Bridge: Vector Graphic Animation via Neural Implicits and Video Diffusion Priors" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">II</span></a> introduces the proposed <span id="S1.p6.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">LINR Bridge</span>. Sec. <a href="#S3" title="III Experiments ‣ LINR Bridge: Vector Graphic Animation via Neural Implicits and Video Diffusion Priors" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">III</span></a> presents experimental results, and Sec. <a href="#S4" title="IV Conclusion ‣ LINR Bridge: Vector Graphic Animation via Neural Implicits and Video Diffusion Priors" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">IV</span></a> provides concluding remarks.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">II </span><span id="S2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Proposed Method</span></h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our method automates the generation of <math id="S2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>-frame animations for a given SVG using a text prompt. This approach consists of three steps: (1) <span id="S2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Vector Graphics Reconstruction</span>: Optimize an LINR to reconstruct the input SVG; (2) <span id="S2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Coarse Animation Generation</span>: Replicate the LINR neural network <math id="S2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> times and optimize them with a text-to-video (T2V) diffusion model with VSDS loss, to generate a coarse LINR animation; (3) <span id="S2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Animation Refinement</span>: Warp or optimize the original SVG to match the LINR animation, resulting in a smooth SVG animation. The pipeline is illustrated in Fig. <a href="#S1.F1" title="Fig. 1 ‣ I Introduction ‣ LINR Bridge: Vector Graphic Animation via Neural Implicits and Video Diffusion Priors" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S2.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">II-A</span> </span><span id="S2.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Vector Graphics Reconstruction</span></h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Implicit neural representation (INR) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite> involves using a neural network to represent image information. Typically, this involves building a neural network to map coordinate inputs <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X,Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X,Y)</annotation></semantics></math> to color outputs <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(R,G,B)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(R,G,B)</annotation></semantics></math>. Since the input coordinate is continuous, this representation produces an image with infinite resolution, which is an important characteristic of SVG. We follow the common practice to use SIREN <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite> as the neural network, which uses sine functions as activation functions to enhance the network’s nonlinear representation ability and therefore image representation capability.
SVG consists of various elements each defined by its own color. To better fit this layered and flat nature, we are inspired by NIVeL <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite> to replace the neural network outputs <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(R,G,B)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(R,G,B)</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(m_{1},m_{2},\dots,m_{L})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(m_{1},m_{2},\dots,m_{L})</annotation></semantics></math>. Here, <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of layers, <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{i}\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{i}\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> represents the coloring intensity for color <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> at the <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th layer. Such a representation is called layered implicit neural representation
(LINR), which enhances the model’s ability to handle layered graphics efficiently.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Specifically, based on the input SVG <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>, we extract its color information based on the stacking order of layers and merge adjacent regions with the same color. We denote the resulting color scheme as <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(c_{1},c_{2},\dots,c_{L})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(c_{1},c_{2},\dots,c_{L})</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> representing the number of layers. We optimize an LINR network <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> to map a coordinate input <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{p}=(X,Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐩</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{p}=(X,Y)</annotation></semantics></math> to an intensity vector <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{m}=(m_{1},m_{2},\dots,m_{L})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐦</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{m}=(m_{1},m_{2},\dots,m_{L})</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{i}\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{i}\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>.
The process can be expressed as <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{m}=f(\mathbf{p})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐦</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐩</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{m}=f(\mathbf{p})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We define <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> as the background color and <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> as the color for the <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th layer of the SVG. The RGB image <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C(f)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C(f)</annotation></semantics></math> represented by a LINR <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is constructed as follows: For all coordinate points <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐩</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{p}</annotation></semantics></math> in the canvas area, we first apply the background color <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, then overlay each layer in order, with the <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th layer contributing color <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> with intensity <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. Here, an intensity of <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> means that no color is applied and the layer is totally transparent at this point, while an intensity of <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> means full color is applied and all colors in the lower layers are covered, and an intensity between <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> represents the opacity of the coloring in this layer, resulting in the RGB image <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C(f)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C(f)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The first stage optimizes LINR <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> to reconstruct the input SVG <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> with the following objectives:</p>
<ul id="S2.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Layered reconstruction loss (<math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{MSE}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>MSE</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{MSE}}</annotation></semantics></math>): Measures the mean squared error between <math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C(f)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C(f)</annotation></semantics></math> and the raster image <math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R(s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(s)</annotation></semantics></math> rendered from <math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> among every position <math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐩</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{p}</annotation></semantics></math> and layer <math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, leading to <math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{MSE}}=\text{MSE}(R(s),C(f))=\mathbb{E}_{\mathbf{p},i}[(s(\mathbf{p})_{i}-f(\mathbf{p})_{i})^{2}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>MSE</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>MSE</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>𝐩</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐩</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐩</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{MSE}}=\text{MSE}(R(s),C(f))=\mathbb{E}_{\mathbf{p},i}[(s(\mathbf{p})_{i}-f(\mathbf{p})_{i})^{2}]</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s(\mathbf{p})_{i}\in\{0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐩</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s(\mathbf{p})_{i}\in\{0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes if position <math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐩</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{p}</annotation></semantics></math> in layer <math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> is colored, and <math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(\mathbf{p})_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐩</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(\mathbf{p})_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the color intensity <math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> at position <math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐩</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{p}</annotation></semantics></math> and layer <math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>.
We use differentiable rasterizer DiffVG <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite> to obtain <math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R(s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(s)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Binarization loss (<math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{bi}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>bi</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{bi}}</annotation></semantics></math>): To maintain the sharp shapes of SVG, we would like the color intensity <math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> to approach the extreme values of <math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>. We penalize <math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B(m_{i})=min\{(m_{i}-0)^{1.1},(1-m_{i})^{1.1}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>1.1</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>1.1</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B(m_{i})=min\{(m_{i}-0)^{1.1},(1-m_{i})^{1.1}\}</annotation></semantics></math> at each position <math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐩</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{p}</annotation></semantics></math> and layer <math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, averaged over all positions and layers for <math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>,
leading to <math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{bi}}=\mathbb{E}_{\mathbf{p},i}[B(f(\mathbf{p})_{i})]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>bi</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>𝐩</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐩</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{bi}}=\mathbb{E}_{\mathbf{p},i}[B(f(\mathbf{p})_{i})]</annotation></semantics></math>. We choose this penalty because when <math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is close to <math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>, the gradient approaches <math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>, and in other regions, the gradient is approximately <math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-1</annotation></semantics></math>. This behavior facilitates optimization and mitigates the risk of gradient explosion.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Regularization loss (<math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{reg}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>reg</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{reg}}</annotation></semantics></math>): Applied to network parameters to prevent gradient explosion with <math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{reg}}=L_{2}(f)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>reg</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{reg}}=L_{2}(f)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S2.SS1.p4.2" class="ltx_p">For reconstruction, we aim to optimize <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> to obtain <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}=\arg\min_{f}\mathcal{L}_{\text{rec}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><msub><mi>min</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>rec</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}=\arg\min_{f}\mathcal{L}_{\text{rec}}</annotation></semantics></math> with</p>
<table id="S2.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{rec}}=\mathcal{L}_{\text{MSE}}+\lambda_{1}\mathcal{L}_{\text{bi}}+\lambda_{2}\mathcal{L}_{\text{reg}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>rec</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>MSE</mtext></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>bi</mtext></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>reg</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{rec}}=\mathcal{L}_{\text{MSE}}+\lambda_{1}\mathcal{L}_{\text{bi}}+\lambda_{2}\mathcal{L}_{\text{reg}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p4.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> are weight parameters.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark.</span> Our proposed LINRs align closely with key SVG characteristics:</p>
<ol id="S2.I2" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S2.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">LINRs support infinite resolution, which aligns perfectly with the resolution independence of SVGs.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">LINRs have layered characteristics that match the natural hierarchical structure of SVGs.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">LINRs mimic the color filling techniques of SVGs for color consistency.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I2.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">4.</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I2.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">LINRs have sharp shape boundaries, paralleling SVGs’ ability to render crisp edges using mathematical curves.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="S2.SS1.p5.2" class="ltx_p">These four properties show how LINRs effectively exploit SVG traits, enabling superior performance in resolution-independent and animation-critical applications.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S2.SS2.6" class="ltx_text">II-B</span> </span><span id="S2.SS2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Coarse Animation Generation</span></h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Video score distillation sampling (VSDS) originates from score distillation sampling (SDS), which was first used to generate 3D models based on an image diffusion model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>, <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite> and can be generalized to SVG <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>. The core idea is to extract prior information from the image diffusion model by comparing the difference between the predicted noise and the added noise. Later, researchers extended image diffusion models to video diffusion models, proposing VSDS for video generation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>, <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>, <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>. However, the large domain gap between SVG and diffusion models makes it extremely hard to directly optimize. Thus, our key idea is to use LINR to bridge the gap.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We replicate the network <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> times to get <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{f}=(f_{0},f_{1},\dots,f_{K-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐟</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{f}=(f_{0},f_{1},\dots,f_{K-1})</annotation></semantics></math> which constructs a static <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>-frame video <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C(\mathbf{f})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐟</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C(\mathbf{f})</annotation></semantics></math>. Next, we optimize <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{f}</annotation></semantics></math> to produce a coarse LINR animation <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{f}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐟</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{f}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> with:</p>
<ul id="S2.I3" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S2.I3.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I3.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I3.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">VSDS loss (<math id="S2.I3.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{VSDS}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>VSDS</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{VSDS}}</annotation></semantics></math>): To ensure visual stability, we propose a new <span id="S2.I3.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">stability extension</span> method to extend <math id="S2.I3.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C(\mathbf{f})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐟</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C(\mathbf{f})</annotation></semantics></math> with a fixed frame of the original image at the beginning (and optionally at the end), resulting in a <math id="S2.I3.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(K+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(K+1)</annotation></semantics></math>-frame video <math id="S2.I3.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V(\mathbf{f})=\text{concat}(R(s),C(\mathbf{f}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐟</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>concat</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐟</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(\mathbf{f})=\text{concat}(R(s),C(\mathbf{f}))</annotation></semantics></math>. This fixed frame serves as an anchor to constrain the appearance of the remaining frames, which effectively ensures visual stability.
We then add noise <math id="S2.I3.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> to these frames at time step <math id="S2.I3.i1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> to get <math id="S2.I3.i1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{\prime}(\mathbf{f})=\alpha_{t}V(\mathbf{f})+\sigma_{t}\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐟</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐟</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{\prime}(\mathbf{f})=\alpha_{t}V(\mathbf{f})+\sigma_{t}\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S2.I3.i1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.I3.i1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> are pre-defined parameters depending on <math id="S2.I3.i1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> and use a T2V diffusion model <math id="S2.I3.i1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{\phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{\phi}</annotation></semantics></math> with text prompt <math id="S2.I3.i1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> to predict noise <math id="S2.I3.i1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{\phi}(V^{\prime}(\mathbf{f}),t,c)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐟</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{\phi}(V^{\prime}(\mathbf{f}),t,c)</annotation></semantics></math>. The VSDS loss is the prediction error: <math id="S2.I3.i1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{VSDS}}=\mathbb{E}_{t}[w(t)(\epsilon_{\phi}(V^{\prime}(\mathbf{f}),t,c)-\epsilon)]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>VSDS</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐟</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{VSDS}}=\mathbb{E}_{t}[w(t)(\epsilon_{\phi}(V^{\prime}(\mathbf{f}),t,c)-\epsilon)]</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S2.I3.i1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(t)</annotation></semantics></math> is a constant depending on <math id="S2.I3.i1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Note that we optimize LINR network parameters with the backpropagated VSDS gradients, which is inherently different from directly optimizing images. LINR parameters determine SVG position and shape, enabling natural motion learning, thanks to our LINR design that closely matches SVGs’ characteristics.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I3.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I3.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I3.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Binarization loss (<math id="S2.I3.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{bi}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>bi</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{bi}}</annotation></semantics></math>): During the animation process, maintaining sharp edges of shapes is still important. We adopt binarization loss in Eq. (<a href="#S2.E1" title="In II-A Vector Graphics Reconstruction ‣ II Proposed Method ‣ LINR Bridge: Vector Graphic Animation via Neural Implicits and Video Diffusion Priors" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) by taking average among <math id="S2.I3.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> frames.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I3.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I3.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I3.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Regularization loss (<math id="S2.I3.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{reg}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>reg</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{reg}}</annotation></semantics></math>): Applied to network parameters to prevent gradient explosion. We adopt regularization loss in Eq. (<a href="#S2.E1" title="In II-A Vector Graphics Reconstruction ‣ II Proposed Method ‣ LINR Bridge: Vector Graphic Animation via Neural Implicits and Video Diffusion Priors" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) by taking average among <math id="S2.I3.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> frames.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">For animation, our goal is to find <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{f}^{*}=\arg\min_{\mathbf{f}}\mathcal{L}_{\text{ani}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐟</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><msub><mi>min</mi><mi>𝐟</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>ani</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{f}^{*}=\arg\min_{\mathbf{f}}\mathcal{L}_{\text{ani}}</annotation></semantics></math> with</p>
<table id="S2.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{ani}}=\mathcal{L}_{\text{VSDS}}+\lambda_{3}\mathcal{L}_{\text{bi}}+\lambda_{4}\mathcal{L}_{\text{reg}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>ani</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>VSDS</mtext></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>bi</mtext></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>reg</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{ani}}=\mathcal{L}_{\text{VSDS}}+\lambda_{3}\mathcal{L}_{\text{bi}}+\lambda_{4}\mathcal{L}_{\text{reg}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{4}</annotation></semantics></math> are weight parameters, respectively.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.07484v1/results.png" id="S2.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:381/125;" width="381" height="125" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 2: </span>Comparisons of <span id="S2.F2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">LINR Bridge</span> to other SVG animating methods. We show consecutive frames of SVG animation. (a) LiveSketch <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite> optimizes global transforms and local point parameters, unsuitable for common vector graphics other than sketches. (b) AniClipart <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite> optimizes an ARAP deformation on vector graphics and offers excessively strict restrictions on shapes. (c) Our method can generate smooth, highly flexible, and well-structured animation results.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S2.SS3.6" class="ltx_text">II-C</span> </span><span id="S2.SS3.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Animation Refinement</span></h3>

<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">After obtaining the LINR animation <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{f}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐟</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{f}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, we warp the original SVG parameters to match <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C(\mathbf{f}^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐟</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C(\mathbf{f}^{*})</annotation></semantics></math>. We first calculate the optical flows from <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R(s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(s)</annotation></semantics></math> to each frame in <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C(\mathbf{f}^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐟</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C(\mathbf{f}^{*})</annotation></semantics></math> using Farneback method <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>. Then we apply the optical flows to every point parameters in <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> to produce a smooth SVG-based animation <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐬</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{s}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For scenarios that require more flexibility and freedom, such as multi-layer ones, we provide an alternative approach: directly optimizing the point parameters of <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> using MSE loss with each frame of LINR animation <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C(\mathbf{f}^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐟</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C(\mathbf{f}^{*})</annotation></semantics></math> to produce <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐬</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{s}</annotation></semantics></math>.
First, we duplicate <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> to obtain <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{s}=(s_{0},s_{1},\dots,s_{K-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐬</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{s}=(s_{0},s_{1},\dots,s_{K-1})</annotation></semantics></math> and then optimize <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐬</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{s}</annotation></semantics></math> with the objectives:</p>
<ul id="S2.I4" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S2.I4.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I4.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I4.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Matching loss (<math id="S2.I4.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{MSE}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>MSE</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{MSE}}</annotation></semantics></math>): Mean squared error loss between the frames <math id="S2.I4.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R(\mathbf{s})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐬</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(\mathbf{s})</annotation></semantics></math> differentiably rendered <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite> from <math id="S2.I4.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐬</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{s}</annotation></semantics></math> and the frames <math id="S2.I4.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C(\mathbf{h^{*}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐡</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C(\mathbf{h^{*}})</annotation></semantics></math> rendered from the corresponding <math id="S2.I4.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{h^{*}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐡</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h^{*}}</annotation></semantics></math>: <math id="S2.I4.i1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{MSE}}=\text{MSE}(R(\mathbf{s}),C(\mathbf{h}^{*}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>MSE</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>MSE</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐬</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐡</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{MSE}}=\text{MSE}(R(\mathbf{s}),C(\mathbf{h}^{*}))</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I4.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I4.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I4.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Structural loss (<math id="S2.I4.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{str}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>str</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{str}}</annotation></semantics></math>):
We follow <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>, <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite> to penalize the difference in triangular mesh-based structure between the optimized SVG and the original SVG, as well as between adjacent SVG frames to increase structural consistency and uniformity of the SVG in the process of optimization.
We denote this loss as <math id="S2.I4.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{str}}=S(\mathbf{s})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>str</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐬</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{str}}=S(\mathbf{s})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S2.SS3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Thus, we seek <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{s}^{*}=\arg\min_{\mathbf{s}}\mathcal{L}_{\text{Fine}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐬</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><msub><mi>min</mi><mi>𝐬</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>Fine</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{s}^{*}=\arg\min_{\mathbf{s}}\mathcal{L}_{\text{Fine}}</annotation></semantics></math> with
These losses are combined with a weight parameter <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{5}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Our goal is to optimize <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐬</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{s}</annotation></semantics></math> to minimize the losses:</p>
<table id="S2.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{Fine}}=\mathcal{L}_{\text{MSE}}+\lambda_{5}\mathcal{L}_{\text{str}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>Fine</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>MSE</mtext></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>str</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{Fine}}=\mathcal{L}_{\text{MSE}}+\lambda_{5}\mathcal{L}_{\text{str}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p2.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{5}</annotation></semantics></math> is a weight parameter.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">III </span><span id="S3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Experiments</span></h2>

<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">III-A</span> </span><span id="S3.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Experiment Setup</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The LINR network is constructed by SIREN network <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite> which has <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math> layers with a hidden dimension of <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="64" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>64</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">64</annotation></semantics></math> and is activated by a clip-sigmoid function where
<math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y=\text{clamp}_{[0,1]}\left((\text{sigmoid}(x)-0.5)\times(1+10^{-7})+0.5\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mtext>clamp</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext>sigmoid</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y=\text{clamp}_{[0,1]}\left((\text{sigmoid}(x)-0.5)\times(1+10^{-7})+0.5\right)</annotation></semantics></math> to prevent large parameters and gradient explosion since a sigmoid function is not able to approach the extreme values of <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>. The differentiably rendered image size is <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="256\times 256" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>256</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>256</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">256\times 256</annotation></semantics></math> pixels.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">During <span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Vector Graphics Reconstruction</span>, <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> increases linearly from <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> over the optimization process—prioritizing overall consistency early and sharp edges later. <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is fixed at <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\times 10^{-6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\times 10^{-6}</annotation></semantics></math>. We use the Adam optimizer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite> with a learning rate of <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\times 10^{-3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\times 10^{-3}</annotation></semantics></math> for 10,000 iterations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For <span id="S3.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Coarse Animation Generation</span>, a 12-frame video is rendered using the ModelScope text-to-video model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>, with timestep <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> randomly sampled between 200 and 400 each iteration. <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{4}</annotation></semantics></math> are set to <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\times 10^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\times 10^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\times 10^{-6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\times 10^{-6}</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively. Adam is used with a learning rate of <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3\times 10^{-5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3\times 10^{-5}</annotation></semantics></math> for 8,000 iterations, saving results every 100 iterations to obtain 80 intermediate LINR animations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In <span id="S3.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Animation Refinement</span>, <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{5}</annotation></semantics></math> is set to <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\times 10^{-7}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\times 10^{-7}</annotation></semantics></math>. Adam is used with a learning rate of <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5\times 10^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5\times 10^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math> for 400 iterations, incorporating intermediate results from the next coarse LINR animation every 5 iterations.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS2.6" class="ltx_text">III-B</span> </span><span id="S3.SS2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Metrics</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We collected approximately 80 SVG vector graphics, covering about 18 human, 24 animals, 6 plants, 22 objects and 10 icons, animated them, and measured the animation results in terms of <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Appearance Consistency</span> and <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Motion-Prompt Alignment</span>. The results are shown in Table <a href="#S3.T1" title="TABLE I ‣ III-C Comparison with State-of-the-Art Methods ‣ III Experiments ‣ LINR Bridge: Vector Graphic Animation via Neural Implicits and Video Diffusion Priors" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">I</span></a>. For <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Appearance Consistency</span>, we measured the CLIP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite> cosine similarity between the generated frames and the original image. For <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Motion-Prompt Alignment</span>, we measured the X-CLIP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite> similarity score between the generated video and the input text prompt.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.07484v1/results3.png" id="S3.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:300/195;" width="300" height="195" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 3: </span>Validating diversity and different prompts. Consecutive frames are selected from animation generated by different prompts.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S3.F4" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.07484v1/fc.png" id="S3.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/87;" width="476" height="87" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 4: </span>Failure cases of direct using of video diffusion models for SVG animation. The leftmost showcase is from SVD <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite> and the others are from CogVideoX <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS3.6" class="ltx_text">III-C</span> </span><span id="S3.SS3.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Comparison with State-of-the-Art Methods</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given the scarcity of automated vector graphic animation methods, we compare our approach with the most related two methods. Example results are shown in Fig. <a href="#S2.F2" title="Fig. 2 ‣ II-B Coarse Animation Generation ‣ II Proposed Method ‣ LINR Bridge: Vector Graphic Animation via Neural Implicits and Video Diffusion Priors" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S3.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Comparison with Direct Use of Video Diffusion Models: Methods like ModelScope <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>, VideoCrafter2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>, DynamiCrafter <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>, I2VGen-XL <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>, SVD <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>, and CogVideoX <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite> rely on video diffusion models trained on natural image domains, lacking constraints on color and shape. Consequently, results often show significant deviations in shape and color, with issues such as distortions and artifacts, shown in Fig. <a href="#S3.F4" title="Fig. 4 ‣ III-B Metrics ‣ III Experiments ‣ LINR Bridge: Vector Graphic Animation via Neural Implicits and Video Diffusion Priors" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Comparison with LiveSketch <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>: LiveSketch is designed for animating sketches. It focuses on global and local optimization by inputting the SVG’s parameter points into an MLP to obtain optimized values, which are then animated using VSDS which leads to shape alterations, unsmooth lines, and excessive freedom. The results are shown in Fig. <a href="#S2.F2" title="Fig. 2 ‣ II-B Coarse Animation Generation ‣ II Proposed Method ‣ LINR Bridge: Vector Graphic Animation via Neural Implicits and Video Diffusion Priors" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>(a).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Comparison with AniClipart <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>: AniClipart extracts key points and skeletons from SVGs, performs As-Rigid-As-Possible (ARAP) deformations based on these key points, and animates using VSDS. SVG motion is constrained by skeleton movements, leading to limited flexibility and excessive joint motion. The results are shown in Fig. <a href="#S2.F2" title="Fig. 2 ‣ II-B Coarse Animation Generation ‣ II Proposed Method ‣ LINR Bridge: Vector Graphic Animation via Neural Implicits and Video Diffusion Priors" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>(b).</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In contrast, our method animates within the LINR domain, ensuring better consistency in color and shape, while providing greater smoothness, freedom of motion, and separation over layers. The results are shown in Fig. <a href="#S2.F2" title="Fig. 2 ‣ II-B Coarse Animation Generation ‣ II Proposed Method ‣ LINR Bridge: Vector Graphic Animation via Neural Implicits and Video Diffusion Priors" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>(c). To further validate the ability of our method in various types of SVGs and different prompts, more results are shown in Fig. <a href="#S3.F3" title="Fig. 3 ‣ III-B Metrics ‣ III Experiments ‣ LINR Bridge: Vector Graphic Animation via Neural Implicits and Video Diffusion Priors" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">TABLE I: </span>Comparisons to Other Methods</figcaption>
<div id="S3.T1.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:345.0pt;height:84.3pt;vertical-align:-39.7pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-1.7pt,0.4pt) scale(0.989992198693757,0.989992198693757) ;">
<table id="S3.T1.2.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S3.T1.2.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_r ltx_border_tt" rowspan="2"><span id="S3.T1.2.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text">Method</span></th>
<th id="S3.T1.2.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_r ltx_border_tt"><span id="S3.T1.2.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Appearance Consistency</span></th>
<th id="S3.T1.2.1.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S3.T1.2.1.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Motion-Prompt Alignment</span></th></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.2.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T1.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_r"><math id="S3.T1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\uparrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↑</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\uparrow</annotation></semantics></math></th>
